plan DiagnosticsContract
title   Canonical diagnostics and failure boundaries
status  archived
goal
    Give consumers one typed diagnostic vocabulary with stable machine data, complete failure
    classification, safe human remediation, and exact terminal JavaScript projections. These are
    proposed target requirements, not shipped behavior.

outcomes
    O1  Canonical Diagnostic schema
        - Diagnostic is the sole package-owned expected failure value. It is a real Error,
        directly yieldable Effect failure, and executable Schema.TaggedError codec with identity
        flow-state/Diagnostic. Classification is exactly Failure, Defect, or Interruption.

        - The exact code/reason sets appear at @O5. The schema fields are:

        | Field | Exact domain |
        | --- | --- |
        | classification | Failure, Defect, or Interruption |
        | code | Closed DiagnosticCode at @O5 |
        | path | Array of strings or finite numbers |
        | details | Record of strings, finite numbers, booleans, or null |
        | summary | string |
        | help | string |

        - These aliases describe exact domains. Except for retained host shapes, they do not
        prescribe additional public exports or a private helper architecture.

        ```ts
        type DiagnosticPath = (string | number)[];
        type FlowPath = DiagnosticPath;
        type DiagnosticScalar = string | number | boolean | null;
        type DiagnosticDetails = Record<string, DiagnosticScalar>;
        type DiagnosticClassification = "Failure" | "Defect" | "Interruption";
        type DiagnosticDocument = Schema.Codec.Encoded<typeof Diagnostic>;
        type DiagnosticProjection = Diagnostic;
        ```

        - DiagnosticPath is (string | number)[]; FlowPath aliases it. Runtime paths accept finite
        numbers. DiagnosticDetails is Record<string, string | number | boolean | null>; numbers
        must be finite. Fields are classification, code, path, details, summary, and help.

        - DiagnosticDocument derives from Schema.Codec.Encoded<typeof Diagnostic>.
        DiagnosticProjection aliases Diagnostic and creates no second model. FlowUsageCode has
        exactly 19 members, including AdmissionOverloaded. Unknown codes reject.

        - InvalidMachineConfigurationReason is closed; path/details name real fields and tokens.
        @V1 proves the schema and exact declarations.

    O2  Typed mappings and complete Cause classification
        - Structural decoding returns Result<A,Diagnostic> without running an Effect or requiring
        services. Translate only SchemaError at that boundary, with issue paths and safe details.
        Decode once; machine compilation consumes the already-decoded value.

        - Semantic validation returns Result<A,Diagnostic>. Expected invalid configuration remains
        data rather than a thrown generic Error. Mapping a semantic failure preserves success A.

        - An owner maps only understood typed failures E to Diagnostic. The resulting Effect
        preserves A and R exactly: Effect<A,E,R> becomes Effect<A,Diagnostic,R>.
        Defects and interruption remain outside E.

        - Preserve complete Exit and Cause until cleanup and evidence aggregation finish.
        Keep Fail, Die, and Interrupt distinguishable. Classification precedence is defect,
        then typed failure, then interruption-only.

        - Do not squash Cause before an intentionally lossy synchronous JavaScript host boundary.
        No convenience throw or Promise rejection becomes a reusable service failure channel.

        - @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4 owns foreign Promise adaptation and final execution boundaries.
        Its rejection mapping produces this same Diagnostic. Supported AbortSignal forwarding
        and cancellation preserve complete Cause through final classification.

        - Later Specs may choose private mapping helpers, aliases, classifier records, and
        completion primitives. Their names, exact helper signatures, and private field layout
        are not consumer contracts. @V2 proves the observable Result/Effect/Cause laws.

    O3  Usage and terminal host projections
        - Only a Failure Diagnostic with a FlowUsageCode may become FlowUsageError at the
        synchronous JavaScript boundary. UsageDiagnostic is notation for that narrowing;
        the private conversion helper name is a later Spec choice.

        - Future FlowUsageError extends Error with _tag FlowUsageError, code, path, details, and
        constructor (code, path?, details?). Exact declarations:

        ```ts
        declare class FlowUsageError extends Error {
          _tag: "FlowUsageError";
          code: FlowUsageCode;
          path: FlowPath;
          details: DiagnosticDetails;
          constructor(code: FlowUsageCode, path?: FlowPath, details?: DiagnosticDetails);
        }
        ```

        ```ts
        type UsageDiagnostic = Diagnostic & { classification: "Failure"; code: FlowUsageCode };
        declare class FlowPersistenceError extends Error {}
        ```

        - FlowPersistenceError is a compatibility-only terminal Error with no additional fields;
        storage, codec, identity, and restoration use canonical Diagnostic internally.

        - @O6 gives 19 maintained remediation rows: failure meaning, illustrative
        path/details/message, and corrective action for every usage code. Consumers branch on
        _tag/code/path/details. Human text may evolve and is never parsed.

        - No public cause member or alternate usage error shape is added. Raw Cause is serialized
        nowhere. Only FlowDisposeError and FlowStoryExecutionError retain complete Cause in
        process, under @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5 and @plan.StoriesAndModels.O3.

        - Package route availability follows @plan.PackageContract.O1; future declarations do not
        restore deferred root names. @V3 proves every usage lane; @V4 proves route boundaries.

    O4  Safe shared rendering
        - Shared diagnostic rendering receives Diagnostic only. Formatting policy remains separate
        from schema fields. Private printer/helper names and signatures are later Spec choices.
        They create no public route, diagnostic service, or second failure model.

        - Emit safe text shaped as error[Code] at path: summary, followed by actionable help:
        text. Escape path/details. Never invent a file, line, or column.

        - ANSI, stacks, raw input, and raw Cause are excluded from renderer input. Machine fields
        remain stable; human prose and whitespace may evolve. @V5 proves safe actionable text.

    O5  Closed diagnostic vocabularies
        - FlowUsageCode is exactly:

        | Usage code |
        | --- |
        | InvalidCanonicalValue |
        | ForeignActorRef |
        | MismatchedActorRef |
        | MissingActorRef |
        | DisposedActorRef |
        | RuntimeNotReady |
        | RuntimeDisposed |
        | MissingContextProvider |
        | ContextDependencyCycle |
        | DuplicateActorClaim |
        | UnadmittedMachine |
        | ActorNotActive |
        | InvalidOperationPlan |
        | WrongOperationKind |
        | OperationNotPending |
        | OperationAlreadySettled |
        | DuplicateStreamDeclaration |
        | BlockedByDependents |
        | AdmissionOverloaded |

        - DiagnosticCode is FlowUsageCode plus exactly:

        | Additional diagnostic code |
        | --- |
        | InvalidMachineConfiguration |
        | SchemaValidation |
        | SemanticValidation |
        | InvalidDescriptorId |
        | DuplicateDescriptorId |
        | DuplicateMachineValue |
        | DuplicateModuleOwnership |
        | MissingModuleReference |
        | InvalidStateDefault |
        | UnresolvedRequirementId |
        | InvalidStoryMetadata |
        | InvalidTraceRecord |
        | InvalidLifecycleTransition |
        | InvalidStoryEvidence |
        | InvalidArtifactOperand |
        | WrongArtifactKind |
        | ArtifactIdentityMismatch |
        | DecompressionFailed |
        | BoundExceeded |
        | StorageFailure |
        | CodecFailure |
        | IdentityVersionMismatch |
        | MalformedData |
        | ConcurrentCapture |
        | NonDurableContextProvider |
        | CleanupFailed |
        | IoFailure |
        | InvariantViolation |
        | Defect |
        | Interruption |

        - InvalidMachineConfigurationReason is exactly:

        | Configuration reason |
        | --- |
        | ConfigurationNotRecord |
        | NonStringConfigurationKey |
        | UnexpectedConfigurationField |
        | StateConfigurationMismatch |
        | ExpectedFunction |
        | UnknownStateToken |
        | UnknownEventToken |
        | InvalidStateToken |
        | TransitionObjectRequired |
        | ExpectedTransition |
        | ExpectedEventHandlers |
        | UnknownEventHandler |
        | EmptyEventHandlerList |
        | EmptyRedirectList |
        | ExpectedRedirect |
        | ExpectedTimer |
        | InvalidTimerDelay |
        | ExpectedActivities |
        | ExpectedStateConfiguration |
        | MissingCompoundStateFields |
        | InvalidDefaultTarget |
        | ExpectedLeafConfiguration |
        | InvalidCompoundState |
        | InvalidTokenTables |
        | AmbiguousHandler |

        - Human summaries/help never add a code or reason. @V1 proves closure and declarations.
        EvidenceUnavailable and UnknownSelector are not DiagnosticCode members. Their exact
        Failure/InvalidArtifactOperand projection is owned by @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O6.
        Their details.reason strings do not widen the closed code set.

    O6  Actionable remediation for every usage code
        - These entries preserve meaning and corrective action. Path/details and messages are
        illustrative. Human wording may evolve; @V3 proves exact 19-code row coverage.

        ### InvalidCanonicalValue

        - Meaning: A canonical key/value contains an unsupported or non-canonical value.
        - Example path/details: path: ["key"]; details: { "reason": "unsupported" }
        - Message illustration: The operation key contains a value that cannot be canonicalized.
        - Corrective action: Replace it with a bounded JSON-safe canonical value.

        ### ForeignActorRef

        - Meaning: The ref belongs to another app or runtime.
        - Example path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "reason": "foreign" }
        - Message illustration: This actor ref belongs to a different runtime.
        - Corrective action: Use a ref created by the receiving app/runtime.

        ### MismatchedActorRef

        - Meaning: The ref is branded for a different machine.
        - Example path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "machine": "Editor" }
        - Message illustration: This actor ref is for a different machine.
        - Corrective action: Pass the exact machine-branded ref required by the operation.

        ### MissingActorRef

        - Meaning: An operation requires an actor ref that was not supplied.
        - Example path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "required": true }
        - Message illustration: An actor ref is required for this operation.
        - Corrective action: Provide the admitted stable ref or use the explicit local-actor path.

        ### DisposedActorRef

        - Meaning: A command addressed an actor after terminal disposal.
        - Example path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "disposed": true }
        - Message illustration: This actor has been disposed and cannot accept commands.
        - Corrective action: Acquire a valid owner/runtime and do not reuse the disposed ref.

        ### RuntimeNotReady

        - Meaning: A handle or command escaped before readiness completed.
        - Example path/details: path: ["runtime"]; details: { "phase": "booting" }
        - Message illustration: The runtime is still booting.
        - Corrective action: Await the sole public `runtime.ready()` Effect before use.

        ### RuntimeDisposed

        - Meaning: A command or lookup targeted a disposed runtime.
        - Example path/details: path: ["runtime"]; details: { "phase": "disposed" }
        - Message illustration: The runtime is disposed and cannot accept work.
        - Corrective action: Construct and own a new runtime execution scope.

        ### MissingContextProvider

        - Meaning: A declared context binding has no admitted provider.
        - Example path/details: path: ["contextBindings", "session"]; details: { "provider":
        "missing" }
        - Message illustration: The required context provider is not admitted.
        - Corrective action: Admit the exact provider ref before constructing the consumer.

        ### ContextDependencyCycle

        - Meaning: Context bindings contain a dependency cycle.
        - Example path/details: path: ["contextBindings"]; details: { "cycle":
        "Session>Theme>Session" }
        - Message illustration: Context bindings contain a dependency cycle.
        - Corrective action: Remove the cycle and keep provider dependencies acyclic.

        ### DuplicateActorClaim

        - Meaning: Two admissions claim one stable actor identity incompatibly.
        - Example path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "claim": "duplicate" }
        - Message illustration: This stable actor is already claimed incompatibly.
        - Corrective action: Join the existing ensure or use a distinct stable identity.

        ### UnadmittedMachine

        - Meaning: A machine is not part of the closed `AppPlan`.
        - Example path/details: path: ["machine"]; details: { "machine": "Editor" }
        - Message illustration: This machine is not admitted by the application plan.
        - Corrective action: Include the machine in the app before runtime construction.

        ### ActorNotActive

        - Meaning: Work was sent while the actor was not active.
        - Example path/details: path: ["actor"]; details: { "lifecycle": "suspended" }
        - Message illustration: The actor is not active and cannot accept this work.
        - Corrective action: Resume through its owning host or wait for runtime activation.

        ### InvalidOperationPlan

        - Meaning: An authored operation plan is structurally invalid.
        - Example path/details: path: ["operation"]; details: { "reason": "invalid-plan" }
        - Message illustration: The operation plan is invalid.
        - Corrective action: Fix the descriptor-owned operation fields before admission.

        ### WrongOperationKind

        - Meaning: A resource, transaction, or stream API received another family.
        - Example path/details: path: ["operation", "kind"]; details: { "expected": "resource" }
        - Message illustration: The operation belongs to a different operation family.
        - Corrective action: Use the API matching the declaration's exact operation kind.

        ### OperationNotPending

        - Meaning: An action requires a pending operation but none is pending.
        - Example path/details: path: ["operation"]; details: { "status": "idle" }
        - Message illustration: This operation is not pending.
        - Corrective action: Start or observe the correct occurrence before acting on it.

        ### OperationAlreadySettled

        - Meaning: A completion/cancellation attempted to settle an occurrence twice.
        - Example path/details: path: ["operation", "occurrence"]; details: { "settled": true }
        - Message illustration: This operation occurrence is already settled.
        - Corrective action: Do not reuse the settled occurrence; issue a new authored attempt.

        ### DuplicateStreamDeclaration

        - Meaning: Two declarations in one actor resolve the same stream descriptor and canonical
        K. This collision includes declarations using distinct slots.
        - Example path/details: path: ["streams", "progress"]; details: { "duplicate": true }
        - Message illustration: This actor already owns a stream for this descriptor and key.
        - Corrective action: Keep one declaration for that actor/descriptor/K. Distinct slots
        alone do not resolve the collision. @plan.Streams.O2 owns this identity rule.

        ### BlockedByDependents

        - Meaning: Disposal would violate active or suspended context dependents.
        - Example path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "dependents": 1 }
        - Message illustration: This actor cannot be disposed while dependents remain bound.
        - Corrective action: Dispose every named dependent through its accepted owner or host
        path before disposing the provider. Existing consumer bindings cannot be rebound.
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6 own these lifetimes.

        ### AdmissionOverloaded

        - Meaning: New work exceeds a finite active command or serialized-work bound.
        - Example path/details: Path/details identify the admission boundary and private capacity
        without payloads.
        - Message illustration: The runtime cannot admit more work at this boundary yet.
        - Corrective action: Wait for accepted work to settle before sending new work.

        - AdmissionOverloaded preserves accepted work and FIFO settlement. Reject the entire new
        finite batch before any occurrence, preview, or adapter work.

decisions
    D1  One diagnostic model owns codes and structured fields
        - Aliases and serialized projections do not create a second expected-error family.
        Capability owners decide when a diagnostic occurs; this Plan owns its vocabulary and
        shape.
    D2  Classification follows complete aggregation
        - Keep complete Cause until classification and cleanup aggregation finish. Serialized
        output carries ordered DiagnosticProjection values, not Cause trees, traversal order, or
        fiber IDs. Independently actionable cleanup diagnostics preserve order and multiplicity.
    D3  Capture conflicts use ConcurrentCapture
        - ConcurrentCapture is the canonical code for retryable capture conflicts.
        ConcurrentDehydrate is a stale historical label, not a runtime alias, exported code, or
        second diagnostic.

constraints
    C1  Do not build another error or execution framework
        - No feature-owned expected Error/Data.TaggedError carrier, diagnostic service,
        Context.Service, Layer, Logger, generic runner, custom Effect wrapper, or second
        Diagnostic codec is added.
    C2  Preserve root deferral
        - FlowPath, FlowUsageCode, FlowUsageError, and FlowPersistenceError remain absent from the
        active root until their owners and proofs permit restoration under
        @plan.PackageContract.O1. @V4 retains the pending history/documentation check before 
        restoration.
    C3  Keep evidence status honest
        - Every executable check below is PLANNED. This proposal reports no compiled declaration,
        runtime proof, packed-consumer result, or product cutover receipt.

verification
    V1  Canonical schema and closed declarations
        - Establishes @O1 and @O5. PLANNED: compile and decode every code/reason/classification
        and malformed fields through the production Diagnostic schema. Reject unknown codes,
        non-finite path or detail numbers, wrong tags, missing fields, and unsafe value domains.

        - Prove Error and directly yieldable Effect behavior, exact document derivation, and no
        alternate projection. Planned checks: nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types
        and nub run test -- packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/diagnostic/test/diagnostic.test.ts.

        - Exact exhaustive tests remain to be implemented; the target declarations remain
        uncompiled.
    V2  Owner mapping, complete Cause, and A/E/R laws
        - Establishes @O2. PLANNED: malformed structure, semantic failures, typed Effect failures,
        mixed Fail/Die/Interrupt, interruption plus cleanup defect, and independent cleanup
        failures.

        - Pass when structure maps SchemaError once, A/R are unchanged, defects remain outside E,
        and precedence uses the complete aggregated Cause. No raw parser exception escapes.
        Planned checks: focused production diagnostic tests and check:types.

        - Typechecks alone cannot prove Cause precedence, cancellation, or cleanup behavior.
    V3  Exhaustive usage failures and corrective actions
        - Establishes @O3 and @O6. PLANNED: trigger all 19 codes through their production
        admission/usage owners, compare each documentation row, and compile stable
        discriminator/path/detail access.

        - Include wrong runtime/machine, missing/disposed refs, context cycles, blocked disposal,
        wrong operation kind, duplicate settlement, duplicate streams, and overloaded admission.

        - Exercise active and suspended dependents during blocked provider disposal. Also declare
        two distinct stream slots with the same actor/descriptor/K. Remedies must use accepted
        owner paths and remove the actual collision; rebind advice or slot renaming fails this 
        proof.

        - Overload preserves accepted FIFO work and rejects a whole new finite batch before work,
        previews, or occurrences. Pass with one row per exact code, actionable text, and no
        cause field, message parsing, new code, or alternate expected-error family.

        - Planned checks: check:types plus production diagnostic/admission tests. Exhaustive
        runtime and documentation-row proof remains unimplemented.
    V4  Deferred declarations and terminal host envelopes
        - Establishes @O3. PLANNED: inspect fresh packed declarations and runtime exports. Pass
        when deferred names remain absent, future declaration shapes remain exact, and only the
        two named terminal host errors can retain raw Cause.

        - Snapshots, selectors, artifacts, and CLI expose none. Planned checks:
        check:packed-consumers and public route tests under
        packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/public/test. These proofs remain pending.
        - Pending restoration gate: verify package history and product documentation for any
        compatibility meaning of existing-boundary wording. No compatibility history has been
        proven. The exact target declarations and current export deferral remain unchanged.

    V5  Safe shared renderers
        - Establishes @O4. PLANNED: render escaped paths/details, hostile control text, each
        classification, and representative corrective actions. Pass with stable code/data,
        actionable help, no fabricated source location, and no raw input, stack, or Cause.

        - Planned checks: focused diagnostic renderer tests; required tests remain unimplemented.

